@ 17.7.2.1.9 - Early Northern Middle English to Scots
# citation: Pogostick Man, from Wikipedia contributors (2014), “Phonological history of Scots”. Wikipedia, the Free Encyclopedia. <https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Phonological_history_of_Scots>; and Wikipedia contributors (2014), “Scottish Vowel Length Rule”. Wikipedia, the Free Encyclopedia. <https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Scottish_vowel_length_rule> /k ɡ/ remain unpalatalized when E_ ul became some sort of diphthong or vowel (possibly one of uː, uw, ʌw), but the article isn’t very clear Vowel shift: Application of the Scottish vowel-length rule:
	b > ∅ / m_l
	t > ∅ / {p,k}_# (“except in some inflected forms” for *kt)
	d > ∅ / n_
	d > ∅ / l_#
	s > ʃ / _E (E_ also?)
	f > ∅ “in certain contexts”
	{ɸ,x} > ∅ / _# (seems to be sporadic)
	ʍ > xw (some speakers seem to have resisted this)
	oɡ > ʌu
	ol al > ou ɑ:[+long]
	ou ɑ:[+long] > ʌu {ɑ,ɔ}
	— ai > ɛi / when stem-final
	ɛi > əi / when stem-final
	— u:[+long] > ʌu / when-stem final, in northern varieties
	— ø:[+long] > wi / {k,ɡ}_ (in Mid Northern dialects)
	— ø:[+long] > i (in northern dialects)
	— ø:[+long] > (j){u,ʌ} / _{k,x} (outcome varies depending upon dialect)
	— a > i / _n (in northern varieties)
	— a > e / _n (otherwise)
	— a > {ɛ,e} / _rC
	— ai oi ui ei au ou iu ɛ(o)u > e:[+long] oe əi i:[+long] {ɑ:[+long],ɔ:[+long]} ʌu ju j(ʌ)u
	— ɛ:[+long] > ɛi ( / in some northern varieties
	ɛi ( > əi?) / in some northern varieties
	— i:[+long] e:[+long] ɛ:[+long] a:[+long] o:[+long] u:[+long] {ø:[+long],y:[+long]} > əi i {i,e} e o u ø
	— æ > ɛ / _C:[+alveolar]
	— a ɔ u > {a,ɑ} ɔ ʌ
	— V > V:[+long] / _{r,F:[+voice],$,#}
	— əi > aɪ / _{r,F:[+voice],$,#} (pursuant to the above)